hexagon: convert Q8_0 K and V rows into the HMX tiles two blocks at a time
Fold-into: upstream/ggml-hexagon

The defect

With a Q8_0 KV cache, the HMX flash attention dequantized each K and V row of a KV block in
VTCM with hvx_dequantize_row_q8_0_f16 before the tile interleave. That function converts one
32-value block per iteration: a scalar load of the scale from VTCM, a splat, an F16-to-F32
pair, two unpacks, an int-to-float conversion, a qf32 multiply and a conversion back to F16,
on a quarter of the vector width. At decode the HMX has almost no work, thus this HVX pass was
the whole op: on the phone the Q8_0 op cost 5.6 times the F16 op for each KV row (127 against
23 ns per row of K and V of one head, 2B, depth 4096 and 16384), and the tg of the 2B at depth
32768 fell from 18.6 (F16 KV) to 10.7 tokens per second. The PMU counters of the op agree:
11.2 million committed packets per op for Q8_0 against 1.0 million for F16, with the user DMA
active in 12 % of the cycles for Q8_0 and in 87 % for F16.

The change

hvx_dequantize_pair_q8_0_f16 (hvx-quant.h) converts two Q8_0 blocks into one vector of 64 F16
values from one unaligned load: a vrdelta takes the 64 quants into bytes 0..63, a vdelta copies
the two scales into their 32 lanes each, then vunpack, the int16-to-F16 conversion, a qf32
multiply and the qf32-to-F16 conversion. There is no scalar load. fa_q8_0_rows_to_tiles and
fa_q8_0_cols_to_tiles (flash-attn-ops.c) write the result straight into the K and the V tiles,
thus the rows need no F16 copy in VTCM. The pair loop has no branch and unrolls. A head
dimension that is not a multiple of 64 keeps the old path.

The values are those of the old path: a quant is exact in F16, the product of two F16 values is
exact in qf32, and the same qf32-to-F16 conversion rounds it. The kernel lab
(tools/htp-lab, target fadq) compares every K and V tile value for random KV blocks (1 to 64
rows, odd tails included): 0 differences. Over all 256 quants and all 63488 finite F16 scales the
only differences are signed zeros for a zero product with a negative or a zero scale, which no
quantizer writes. The lab measures 66 cycles for a row of 256 values against 656 (v79, timing
mode, the release flags).

The result on the phone (HTP0, the 2B, tg32, all runs at the same CPU clock caps): with a Q8_0
cache the decode gives 15.14, 16.28 and 17.12 tokens per second at the depths 4096, 16384 and
32768, against 14.12, 14.75 and 12.83 with an F16 cache. The old library gave 8.99 at 16384.
The KL of the 2B against the naive x86 oracle at 4096 and 16384 tokens is the same as with the
old library in each row of the table, to six decimals. A greedy generation with the 2B and with
the 4B gives the correct answer.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

